   "text": "The pattern fits a single B-site-ordered, distorted double perovskite, and Ca2FeWO6 (mp-1276937) is the best match.\n\n1. **Perovskite parent peaks.** There are peaks at about 22.55/22.85\u00b0, 32.25\u00b0, 45.8\u201346.7\u00b0, 51.6\u201352.6\u00b0 and 57.2\u201357.9\u00b0. Their d-spacings are about 3.94/3.89, 2.774, 1.98\u20131.94, 1.75 and 1.60 \u00c5. These index as pseudo-cubic (100), (110), (200), (210) and (211), giving a cell edge a_p \u2248 3.92 \u00c5.\n\n2. **Ordering peaks.** There are clear peaks at 19.6\u00b0 (d = 4.53 \u00c5), 37.9/38.3\u00b0 (d \u2248 2.37 \u00c5) and 50.7\u00b0 (d \u2248 1.80 \u00c5). These index as (111), (311) and (331) of the doubled cell (a \u2248 7.84 \u00c5). They mark rock-salt ordering of two different B-site cations. The (111) peak is about as strong as the main perovskite peak, which points to a large scattering contrast between the two B cations, such as Fe versus W.\n\n3. **Splitting.** The parent peaks are split, so the cell is lower than cubic.\n\n4. **Comparing candidates.**\n   - Ca2FeWO6 is an ordered, distorted double perovskite. Its cell volume gives a_p \u2248 3.98 \u00c5, about 1.5% larger than observed, which is the closest of the candidates.\n   - Na2ZrWO6 is ordered but cubic, with a_p \u2248 4.05 \u00c5. Its peaks would fall about 3.5% too low in d-spacing, and cubic symmetry cannot explain the splitting.\n   - NaNdTi2O6 has the right cell size (a_p \u2248 3.88 \u00c5) but only Ti on the B site. It could not give a strong (111) ordering peak, and its A-site layering peak near 11.4\u00b0 is absent.\n\n5. **Second phase.** I found no significant peaks left over, so I see no evidence for a second phase.\n\n{\"present\": [\"mp-1276937\"], \"confidence\": 0.72, \"note\": \"Peaks index to a distorted, B-site-ordered double perovskite (a_p about 3.92 \u00c5) with strong (111)/(311)/(331) ordering peaks, which fits Ca2FeWO6 better than the cubic, larger-cell Na2ZrWO6 or the all-Ti NaNdTi2O6.\"}",
